{"content": [{"text": "I shouldn't use tools for this task — the instructions say to produce the answer directly as text. Let me work it out by hand carefully.\n\nI'll use a DP over positions tracking (running sum $s$, last digit $d$). I'll compute the count tables by hand, layer by layer, using the recurrence\n\n$$f_{k+1}(d', s) = g_k(s-d') - f_k(d', s-d'),$$\n\nwhere $f_k(d,s)$ = number of valid length-$k$ prefixes ending in digit $d$ with sum $s$, and $g_k(s) = \\sum_d f_k(d,s)$.\n\nDoing all 10 digits across sums 0–30 for 7 layers by hand is enormous and error-prone. Let me instead use a **symmetry reduction plus generating-function inclusion-exclusion** that I can verify.\n\n**Key symmetry.** The map $d \\mapsto 9-d$ is a bijection on digits that preserves the \"no equal adjacent\" constraint and sends sum $S$ to $63-S$. So $N(30) = N(33)$, and more usefully it confirms the distribution is symmetric about $31.5$. I'll use this only as a sanity anchor.\n\n**Method: transfer over a reduced statistic.** Let $A_k(s)$ = number of valid length-$k$ strings with sum $s$ (no equal adjacent), i.e. $g_k(s)$. I need the per-last-digit data to step forward, so I really must carry $f_k(d,s)$. I'll exploit that I only ever need sums up to 30 and that low/high digits are constrained.\n\nLet me instead reformulate as a cleaner inclusion–exclusion that avoids tracking last digit at all.\n\n**Counting strings with sum 30, digits 0–9, no two equal adjacent.**\n\nLet $U$ = number of length-7 strings, digits 0–9, sum 30, with **no adjacency constraint**. Then subtract those having at least one equal-adjacent pair, via inclusion–exclusion over the set of \"bad\" adjacency positions. But \"equal adjacent\" pairs aren't independent linear constraints, so standard I-E over positions is messy because a run of equal digits collapses variables.\n\nThe clean way: I-E over the set $B \\subseteq \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$ of positions $i$ where we *force* $d_i = d_{i+1}$. For a forced set $B$, the positions split into \"blocks\" of consecutive indices glued by equalities; if $B$ has the equalities forming $b$ glued bonds, the 7 variables collapse into $7-b$ free variables (each block is a single value repeated). A block of length $\\ell$ (i.e. $\\ell$ equal consecutive digits) contributes a variable $x$ with the block summing to $\\ell x$.\n\nBy inclusion–exclusion (Möbius over forced-equality subsets), the count of strings with **no** equal adjacency is\n\n$$N = \\sum_{B \\subseteq \\{1..6\\}} (-1)^{|B|} \\,\\Big(\\text{\\# digit-assignments to blocks with weighted sum } 30\\Big).$$\n\nGiven $B$, the string is partitioned into consecutive blocks of lengths $\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_m$ (with $\\sum \\ell_j = 7$, $m = 7-|B|$), and $|B| = 7-m = \\sum(\\ell_j-1)$. Each block gets a digit $x_j \\in \\{0,\\dots,9\\}$, contributing $\\ell_j x_j$ to the sum. We need $\\sum_j \\ell_j x_j = 30$. The sign is $(-1)^{7-m}$.\n\nSo:\n$$N = \\sum_{\\substack{\\text{compositions }(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_m)\\\\ \\text{of } 7}} (-1)^{7-m} \\cdot C(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_m),$$\nwhere $C(\\ell_1,\\dots,\\ell_m) = \\#\\{(x_1,\\dots,x_m)\\in\\{0..9\\}^m : \\sum \\ell_j x_j = 30\\}$.\n\nSince $C$ depends only on the **multiset** of block lengths, I'll group compositions by their integer-partition type of 7, count how many compositions have that type, and multiply.\n\n**Block-length partition types of 7** (with $m$ = number of parts, sign $(-1)^{7-m}$):\n\nLet me enumerate partitions of 7 and the number of *ordered* compositions of each.\n\n| Partition of 7 | parts $m$ | sign $(-1)^{7-m}$ | # compositions (orderings) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7 | 1 | $(-1)^6=+$ | 1 |\n| 6+1 | 2 | $-$ | 2 |\n| 5+2 | 2 | $-$ | 2 |\n| 4+3 | 2 | $-$ | 2 |\n| 5+1+1 | 3 | $+$ | 3 |\n| 4+2+1 | 3 | $+$ | 6 |\n| 3+3+1 | 3 | $+$ | 3 |\n| 3+2+2 | 3 | $+$ | 3 |\n| 4+1+1+1 | 4 | $-$ | 4 |\n| 3+2+1+1 | 4 | $-$ | 12 |\n| 2+2+2+1 | 4 | $-$ | 4 |\n| 3+1+1+1+1 | 5 | $+$ | 5 |\n| 2+2+1+1+1 | 5 | $+$ | 10 |\n| 2+1+1+1+1+1 | 6 | $-